            <article class="article-content">
                <p>
                    In investigative journalism, metadata hidden in PDF documents can reveal critical information about document provenance, editing history, and authorship. Understanding how to read and interpret PDF metadata is a skill that separates rigorous verification from speculation.
                </p>

                <h2>#1. Why PDF Metadata Matters in Journalism</h2>
                <p>
                    When a source provides a leaked document, PDF metadata tells the story of its creation. The modification dates, author name, editing software, and revision count can help answer crucial questions: Is this document authentic? Has it been edited since publication? Who originally created it?
                </p>

                <div class="highlight-box">
                    <strong>Key Finding:</strong> PDF metadata can expose author identity even when documents are anonymized in other ways. Journalists must remove metadata before publishing sensitive source material.
                </div>

                <h2>#2. What Metadata Can Tell You</h2>
                <p>Common fields to examine when verifying documents:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Author/Creator:</strong> The person or system that created the file</li>
                    <li><strong>Subject & Keywords:</strong> Clues about document classification or internal purpose</li>
                    <li><strong>Creation Date:</strong> When the document was originally made</li>
                    <li><strong>Modification History:</strong> How many times edited and by whom (in some PDFs)</li>
                    <li><strong>Producer:</strong> Software used to create or modify the PDF</li>
                    <li><strong>Company/Organization:</strong> Corporate metadata embedded by the authoring organization</li>
                </ul>

                <h2>#3. Protecting Your Sources</h2>
                <p>
                    Before publishing any document provided by a source, always strip metadata. Even when a source believes they've been careful, embedded metadata can compromise anonymity.
                </p>
                <p>
                    FileIntel's PDF Metadata Remover inspects every metadata field in seconds and removes all author identification, timestamps, and software traces with a single click—no server uploads, no logging, 100% private.
                </p>

                <h2>#4. A newsroom verification workflow</h2>
                <p>
                    Treat metadata as one evidence stream, not as proof by itself. Preserve the original file in a restricted evidence directory, record its cryptographic hash, and work from a copy. Note the filename, source context, acquisition time, and transfer method before opening the document in a desktop application that might rewrite it.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Start with a read-only inspection. Compare CreationDate and ModDate, identify the Creator and Producer applications, and check whether the document contains an XMP packet, embedded files, comments, form data, or incremental updates. A date mismatch can suggest an editing sequence, but it cannot establish who edited a file or whether the file is authentic.
                </p>
                <div class="highlight-box">
                    <strong>Verification rule:</strong> Never publish a conclusion based on one metadata field. Corroborate metadata with document content, visual comparison, file structure, independent records, and the source's account of how the file was obtained.
                </div>

                <h2>#5. Source communication and device hygiene</h2>
                <p>
                    SecureDrop and Signal solve different problems. SecureDrop is designed for anonymous source submissions to participating newsrooms; Signal provides encrypted messaging but does not make a journalist's device, screenshots, downloads, or backups anonymous. Use the newsroom's established intake policy rather than asking a source to send sensitive material through a personal account or an unapproved cloud drive.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Tails can reduce local traces when it is correctly configured, but it is not a magic anonymity layer and it does not repair an already exposed document. Journalists should follow their organisation's threat model, keep operating systems and browsers updated, use multi-factor authentication, and avoid opening source files on a personal device that is also used for public social accounts. Consult a newsroom security specialist for high-risk investigations.
                </p>

                <h2>#6. Remove metadata from the publication copy</h2>
                <p>
                    The file used for verification is not the file that should be uploaded to a publication system. After the reporting team has documented the original, create a separate publication copy. Remove author, company, keyword, timestamp, software, comments, attachments, hidden layers, and other fields that could identify a source or reveal internal workflow. Then inspect the exported copy again with a second tool.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Metadata removal does not anonymise visible text, image content, document layout, or distinctive language. A cropped screenshot can still contain a location clue, and a PDF can still expose a source through names, revision marks, or embedded images. Redact or recreate sensitive content separately, and ask another editor to review the final file before publication.
                </p>

                <h2>#7. Best practices</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li>Always inspect metadata before publishing—treat it as part of your source verification checklist</li>
                    <li>Remove metadata from documents before publication using a verified tool</li>
                    <li>Document the metadata you find as part of your verification notes (for internal use only)</li>
                    <li>Remember: metadata removal is not the same as redaction. Use redaction for sensitive content within the document itself</li>
                    <li>Keep the original evidence separate from the sanitised publication copy</li>
                    <li>Have a second editor review the file and its visible content before release</li>
                </ul>

